# ORDER MATTERS HERE, and it is the opposite of what reads naturally: in
# gitattributes the LAST matching pattern wins, so the catch-all must come
# first and the specific overrides after it. Written the other way round -- the
# specific rules on top, the `*` line at the bottom -- the catch-all silently
# wins and every override below is dead. That is not hypothetical: it is the
# bug this file shipped with, and CI caught it on a fresh clone while the
# authoring machine kept the right bytes on disk and saw nothing wrong.

# Default: normalize to LF in the repository and check out LF. Git's usual
# behaviour, stated explicitly so a contributor's core.autocrlf cannot change it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows entry points must be checked out CRLF on every platform, including the
# Linux runner that CI uses. cmd.exe does not reliably tolerate a bare LF before
# a `goto :label`, and PowerShell's param() block and here-strings are likewise
# sensitive. tests/test_launcher.py asserts CRLF on these three, so this rule is
# load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
